    question_en: "A healthcare startup named CardioPulse Health operates an application on AWS that ingests and processes high-resolution cardiac scans and patient diagnostic records. The backend application runs on Amazon EC2 instances located in private subnets across multiple Availability Zones. The EC2 instances process raw scans (ranging from 200 MB to 1.5 GB in size) and store the final artifacts in an Amazon S3 bucket.\n\nThe startup has identified two major cost drivers on their monthly AWS bill:\n- Substantial data processing charges from the NAT Gateway used by private EC2 instances to upload and retrieve scan files to and from Amazon S3.\n- Rapidly escalating S3 storage costs. Medical scans are accessed frequently during the first 30 days following an exam, but access drops sharply after 30 days. However, scans must remain immediately available in real-time when requested by doctors. Additionally, network interruptions during large file uploads have left abandoned multipart upload parts accumulating in the bucket.\n\nWhich combination of actions should a solutions architect recommend to MOST cost-effectively address these issues? (Choose two.)",
